  abstract = {Environmental factors can lead to heritable non-genetic changes in the mammalian germ line that affect behaviour in the offspring across several generations. These changes influence behaviours related to human neuropsychiatric disease and higher cognitive functions.Experimental strategies in laboratory rodents can be used to test the germline dependence of heritable changes in behaviour.Novel non-genetic mechanisms such as epigenetic marks and non-coding RNAs have emerged as likely vectors for germline transmission.The routes and mechanisms by which environmental factors can affect non-genetic factors in germ cells are discussed.Major questions remain to be addressed, including the identification of non-genetic modifications that are altered by the environment in germ cells, the mechanisms of their induction and maintenance during development and adulthood, and the way in which they affect behaviour.},
